#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096394 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "f1021ccd-e37f-5880-baf2-0f0eacd7dd95" :question/math-academy-id "e-481" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{{\\int}} \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin {2\\theta} =2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\theta) = \\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}\n$$\n\nLetting\n\n$$\n\\theta= \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sin \\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right) &= \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin x &= \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(-\\cos {x}\\right)+C \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cos {x}+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "99b7b82d-431f-5992-9b1e-a80a6b3850c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-482" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\cos^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\cos^2{x}$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2 x -1\n$$\n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\cos^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int (\\cos {2x} +1) \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {2x} +x\\right) + C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin {2x} +\\dfrac{1}{2}x + C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "6269e793-cee7-59d6-8656-12a5a7475f2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-483" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin^2(3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term:\n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right)=\\sin^2\\theta\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right)=\\sin^2(3x)\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\sin^{2} (3x)dx &= ∫\\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 6x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1 - \\cos 6x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x - \\frac{1}{6}\\sin 6x) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{1}{12}\\sin 6x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "55a54d74-449e-5720-b808-9f275b678b2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-484" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta -\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos 2\\theta\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x = \\cos 6x\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} {\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\cos 6x\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\dfrac{1}{6}\\sin6x+ C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a038f1d3-fd1c-5a25-a854-84ee139c9b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x - 5 + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093502} {:answer/id #uuid "175c4d9c-8b77-51df-93e9-3a9d2627a82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x - 5x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093503} {:answer/id #uuid "3612f581-efc3-5949-b943-dd4cabb70a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x - 5x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093504} {:answer/id #uuid "52328aa8-f6fc-52ea-9e60-c67ab5bb8c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x - 5x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093505} {:answer/id #uuid "662df38f-55e6-5818-a574-599928b3b854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x - 5x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3612f581-efc3-5949-b943-dd4cabb70a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x - 5x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093504} :answer-field/id #uuid "4225b174-e4e9-5de3-8b24-d05d3f1140dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093501}] :question/id #uuid "c7507030-351a-513f-a28e-962e017050ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-10423" :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$∫(\\sin x\\cos x - 5)dx$."}]}
